I went looking through old websites expecting a little technological nostalgia. Instead, I found the same question I am working on now: how should information be arranged so someone can find it, understand it and connect it to something else?

The record I can recover runs from 2002 to 2026. The tools changed from static HTML and Flash to content models, semantic embeddings and server-rendered SVG. The concern stayed surprisingly consistent.

> **tl;dr**
>
> - My recoverable web history now spans 24 years, with a lost GeoCities page somewhere before it.
> - Early sites, blog posts and photographs already show the same interests in search, information graphics and typography that appear in my current work.
> - The Story Lab made that continuity easier to see, but it also exposed how much sits outside the current archive.
> - The next step is not to put everything in one feed. It is to make this site an index across the pieces.

## The evidence was already there

My first website was a GeoCities page I made in high school around 1997. I may never find it again. My memory is that I first edited HTML in HotDog, a Windows editor [released in 1995](https://www.webdesignmuseum.org/software/hotdog-1-0-in-1995).

The first substantial site I can recover is Environmental Media. The Wayback Machine kept a [2002 snapshot of the site](https://web.archive.org/web/20020120145620/https://envmedia.com/), which I built in 2001 with search, Flash video and an online shop. The implementation is very much of its time; the interest in helping people navigate different kinds of information is less so.

An [October 2002 snapshot of Stuffed in a Box](https://web.archive.org/web/20021012212136/http://www.stuffedinabox.com/) preserves a mostly "Coming Soon" site for my tiny web-design service. By April 2003, an [online résumé](https://web.archive.org/web/20030425093346/http://www.stuffedinabox.com/portfolio/resume/index.htm) listed Stuffed in a Box Design as a self-owned business and used proper em dashes. For the record, the dash habit predates generative AI by more than twenty years.

The blog arrived on [5 September 2003](https://web.archive.org/web/20050206010037/http://www.stuffedinabox.com/archives/000004.html). Its first post, "I've done and will do," promised observations about newspapers, infographics, technology, politics and history. Not much happened after that. A post the following September admitted that it had been nearly a year since the last one, but the intended subjects are recognizable.

Flickr supplies another breadcrumb. In December 2006 I uploaded [a close-up of a pinched letterform](https://www.flickr.com/photos/khawkins04/337710045/). It does not establish a grand intellectual lineage, but it gives the later typography experiments a longer prehistory: I was already stopping to admire ink traps in 2006.

Individually, these are ordinary fragments from an early web career. Together, they point toward a recurring concern: how information is structured, how it looks and how people move through it.

## Reading the archive instead of scrolling it

The archive on this site starts much later, in 2013. It now contains roughly a hundred entries written on and off across different jobs, tools and versions of the site. That is enough material to compare topics and find passages that unexpectedly resemble one another. It is also enough material for a conventional reverse-chronological blog to hide most of what is there.

The redesign began with typography, graph paper and a more technical datasheet vocabulary. Posts gained document references. The [archive gained a timeline](/archive/), while [topic pages](/topics/) and small activity charts made it possible to browse by subject rather than only by publication date.

Those details changed the basic question of the site. A blog normally asks, "What did I publish most recently?" A register asks, "What is here, and how does one thing relate to another?"

The newest entry is not necessarily the best way into the work. A [2017 post about information architecture](/posts/20170723-corporate-design-through-ia.html) leads more naturally to a [2026 argument about content delivery](/posts/20260327-content-architecture-shipping-container/) than to whatever happened to be published between them. A [type experiment from 2018](/posts/20180319-data-font-for-life-sciences.html) makes more sense beside later work on [data fonts](/posts/20260512-datatype-data-font-inside-out/) and [embedded SVG text](/posts/20260429-svgomg-font/).

The [Corpus Story Lab](/stats/story-lab/) tests those readings. One view looks for ideas that return after several years. Radial diagrams compare links I added with relationships suggested by passage embeddings. Another view assembles "biographies" from a few editorial anchor posts and the entries closest to them in meaning.

That typography biography currently begins with writing from 2017 and 2018. The Flickr photograph shows why the larger archive matters: the visualization is not wrong, but its corpus is incomplete.

## Similarity is a prompt, not a conclusion

Semantic search finds repeated language and adjacent subject matter. It does not show that one idea caused another, that an employer shaped a topic, or even that I consciously remembered an older post while writing a newer one.

The embeddings measure textual proximity. I choose the lens, the threshold and what the pattern means.

The results tend to fall into three groups:

- a connection I already made explicit with a link;
- an older technical concern and a newer organizational argument that share a vocabulary;
- a plausible pair that falls apart as soon as I read both pieces.

The first is a useful sanity check. The second gives me a path worth following. The third shows where similarity stops being useful.

The Story Lab therefore does two jobs. It surfaces possible continuities, and it makes the gaps in the underlying collection harder to ignore. The second may be more valuable than the first.

## The site can become the index

Much of the evidence still lives elsewhere:

- more than a thousand photographs in [my Flickr archive](https://www.flickr.com/photos/khawkins04/), which should not remain their only home;
- writing published through employers, projects and publications that now lives on sites I do not control;
- small tools and experiments spread across [my GitHub repositories](https://github.com/khawkins98);
- older versions of this site preserved unevenly in Git history and the Wayback Machine.

None of those collections is the same kind of thing. A photograph is not a post. A repository is not an essay. A work artifact needs context that a commit log cannot provide. Bringing them together should not mean pouring everything into one chronological feed and calling it an archive.

The role I want for this site is an index across them:

- photograph records with dates, captions and links back to their original context;
- project records connecting a GitHub repository to the problem it tried to solve, related writing and whatever eventually happened to it;
- recovered articles with clear provenance for where they first appeared.

It also means accepting gaps. I do not want to manufacture a continuous personal history from incomplete evidence. "I cannot find this" is useful archive metadata. So is "this only survives as a screenshot" or "the original link is gone."

Photography is the part I most want to bring back as an active practice, not only a rescue operation. The current archive is heavily weighted toward words and software. More photographs would change both the texture of individual posts and the stories the site can tell over time.

The immediate work is practical:

1. keep refining the Story Lab until a few views earn a permanent place;
2. create a useful inventory of writing, photographs and projects that live elsewhere;
3. recover selectively, with source links and dates intact;
4. add structures for photographs and project records instead of forcing them into blog-post templates;
5. make the archive easier to export so this exercise does not have to be repeated from scratch.

The site has been rebuilt several times: Drupal, Gulp, Panini, Eleventy, then a much simpler Eleventy stack. Each migration focused on the machinery. Looking across 24 years changes the emphasis.

The through line is not a particular platform or even a continuous publishing habit. It is the repeated attempt to make information legible, navigable and connected. The point of the archive is to preserve enough context to see that pattern, then enough structure to keep following it.

If you have recovered an old personal site, photo archive or scattered set of projects, I would be interested in what you preserved, what you left where it was, and what was already gone by the time you looked.

